## Analysis and placement

Each reads a map: what can be reached, where to start and end, and what lies near the way between {% own /%}. The talk’s next set of algorithms all rely on Dijkstra maps {% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s209" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s210" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s211" /%}. The last card, the locked door, is a rule of our own for what the talk describes without code {% own /%}.

{% card title="Culling what can’t be reached" id="cull" kind="Analysis" %}
Find the parts of a map that can’t be reached, then delete them or put them to use {% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s222" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s223" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s230" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s231" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s232" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s233" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s234" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-slides/pdf#p24" /%}.

Stage `cull`. The talk’s demo: `c16-unreachable`.

{% part title="The rule" %}
1. Find the open tile nearest the center of the map {% cite id="wolverson2020-code/c16-unreachable/src/main.rs#L36-L54" /%}.
2. Make a Dijkstra map from that tile: every tile it can walk to gets its number of steps {% cite id="wolverson2020-code/c16-unreachable/src/main.rs#L62-L63" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s214" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s215" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s216" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s217" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s218" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s219" /%}.
3. Open tiles left without a number can’t be reached {% cite id="wolverson2020-code/c16-unreachable/src/main.rs#L64-L69" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s226" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s227" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s228" /%}.
4. By mode:
   - `mark`: color those tiles and leave them open {% cite id="wolverson2020-code/c16-unreachable/src/main.rs#L64-L69" /%};
   - `remove`: turn them to wall {% cite id="wolverson2020-code/c17-starting-left/src/main.rs#L55-L65" /%}.

{% figure src="assets/rules/cull-flood-light.png" dark="assets/rules/cull-flood-dark.png" alt="A small cave with one tile numbered 0. Every tile that can be walked to from it carries its number of steps, up to 5. A pocket of two open tiles at the right is walled off; it has no number and is marked." caption="A Dijkstra map from one tile (0): each tile it can walk to gets its number of steps. The pocket at the right gets none, so it can’t be reached (small squares)." /%}

- **Talk says:** to make a Dijkstra map, start from one or more points and mark the rest of the map with a sentinel value, meaning you can’t go there. Then number every walkable tile by its steps from the start {% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s214" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s215" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s216" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s217" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s218" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s219" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s220" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s221" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-slides/pdf#p23" /%}.
{% /part %}

{% part title="Step-through" %}
{% interactive id="lab" caption="Step through Caves, unreachable marked: seed 1, 80 by 50 tiles. Change the seed or a control and the map is built again." params={mode: "card", stage: "cull", demos: ["c16-unreachable"]} %}
{% steps %}
{% step src="assets/steps/c16-0011-light.png" dark="assets/steps/c16-0011-dark.png" alt="Caves, unreachable marked, seed 1: the map at step 11 of 13." at=11 of=13 %}
The map before the next step.
{% /step %}
{% step src="assets/steps/c16-0012-light.png" dark="assets/steps/c16-0012-dark.png" alt="Caves, unreachable marked, seed 1: the map at step 12 of 13." key="cull.center" at=12 of=13 %}
**Center:** the open tile nearest the center of the map is found {% cite id="wolverson2020-code/c16-unreachable/src/main.rs#L36-L54" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s224" /%}.
{% /step %}
{% step src="assets/steps/c16-0013-light.png" dark="assets/steps/c16-0013-dark.png" alt="Caves, unreachable marked, seed 1: the map at step 13 of 13." key="cull.marked" at=13 of=13 %}
**Marked:** a Dijkstra map from that tile gives every tile it can walk to a distance. Open tiles left without one can’t be reached {% cite id="wolverson2020-code/c16-unreachable/src/main.rs#L62-L73" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s225" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s226" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s227" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s228" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s229" /%}. Tiles marked: {% value %}14{% /value %}.
{% /step %}
{% /steps %}

On the command line: `map-lab build recipes/c16-unreachable.json`.
{% /interactive %}
{% /part %}

{% part title="Controls" %}
- `mode` (`remove`, `mark`; the talk’s: `mark`) {% own /%}. `mark`: shows the unreachable tiles and leaves them, as the demo `c16-unreachable` does {% cite id="wolverson2020-code/c16-unreachable/src/main.rs#L62-L73" /%}. `remove`: turns them to wall, as the demo `c17-starting-left` does before it places its start {% cite id="wolverson2020-code/c17-starting-left/src/main.rs#L54-L94" /%}.
{% /part %}

