{% /card %}

{% card title="Placing the start" id="start" kind="Analysis" %}
Decide where the start should be, then put it on the nearest tile that is open {% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s241" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s242" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s243" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-slides/pdf#p25" /%}.

Stage `start`. The talk’s demo: `c17-starting-left`.

{% part title="The rule" %}
1. Choose a desired point:
   - `left`: the middle of the left edge {% cite id="wolverson2020-code/c17-starting-left/src/main.rs#L70" /%};
   - `center`: the center of the map {% cite id="wolverson2020-code/c19-ending-dijkstra/src/main.rs#L69" /%}.
2. The start goes on the open tile nearest that point, in a straight line {% cite id="wolverson2020-code/c17-starting-left/src/main.rs#L69-L86" /%}.

- **On a culled map:** the demo culls first, so the start can walk to everything that is left {% cite id="wolverson2020-code/c17-starting-left/src/main.rs#L34-L67" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s236" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s237" /%}.
- **Talk says:** place somewhere in the middle on the left, then look around for a neighboring tile that is open and hasn’t been culled {% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s241" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s242" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s243" /%}.
{% /part %}

